  "rationale": "Self-describing column names (<LINE> Initial/Final 1-4, Male/Female Mouse 1-4); the two GSMs (Part1, Part2) each hold the libraries of several lines, so samples carry the GSM of their part. The paper scores each gene by the mean of its five guides' fold changes against the paired initial culture, z-scores them against the control guides, and compares in vivo (8 xenografts) with in vitro (4 cultures after two weeks) by t-test per line (Fig. 1b). The BARCS equivalent is xenograft tumors vs final cultures within each line (4 vs 8 libraries, 10 residual df); the shared initial library cancels in that contrast. Tumors are not stated to derive from specific cultures, so the comparison is unpaired. Controls are the 50 control sgRNAs (ControlA1-ControlJ5), which behave as non-targeting (they rise in final cultures as targeting guides deplete); fewer than 100 controls, so the control null is not available. Ribosomal-protein positive-control sgRNAs (sgRPL11_1 ..) are assigned to their genes. Median-ratio totals for the tumor analyses: in tumors a few guides (PTEN, AP2 subunits) take up to 94% of a library's reads, so library totals would call most genes depleted; median ratios over the 660 guides are robust to that. A second analysis, final vs initial cultures across all lines (~ culture + stage, pairing each final culture with its own initial culture, 20 pairs), checks in vitro essentiality (AP2, ribosomal positive controls); it uses control totals because the paper zeroes each sample on its control guides and the controls rise as targeting guides deplete in culture.",
